# Package and document the first release

## Goal

Publish reproducible, verifiable binaries and enough operational guidance for a
user to install, configure, upgrade, and remove the server safely.

## Required work

- Define supported operating systems and architectures from evidence.
- Build reproducible release artifacts and checksums.
- Include license, third-party notices, and provenance manifests.
- Document Bridge setup, secret storage, Codex configuration, and removal.
- Define compatibility and migration policy.
- Gate release creation on every advertised tool succeeding through the
  installed binary and a real MCP client on the primary visible-login path.

## Acceptance criteria

- Fresh-machine installation is exercised on every claimed platform.
- Fresh-machine evidence starts without a Rust/Node toolchain or browser
  automation runtime. Bridge prerequisites are detected and documented
  explicitly rather than hidden behind a fallback.
- Release archives contain no credentials or local reference corpus.
- Checksums and exact source revision are published.
- Upgrade and uninstall preserve the user's mailbox and secrets.
- A new user can install with one documented command, authenticate normally,
  register the MCP server with one action, and use it without source checkout.
- The release does not advertise a capability, platform, or remote deployment
  that lacks its named acceptance transcript.
- `TODO.md` has no remaining open record when the first supported release is
  declared; mock-only or skipped live evidence fails this criterion.

## Evidence

- Release workflow and manifest
- Installation transcripts
- Artifact-content audit
- Redacted end-to-end release acceptance transcript
